{
  "biological_interpretation": "KCC2/SLC12A5 loss can be read as a pro-excitability perturbation in a single thalamic glutamatergic state, but the WNK-SPAK/NKCC1/KCC2 regulatory pathway is not represented as a shared steady-state RNA signature under the sampled conditions.",
  "caveats": [
    "The WNK/SPAK/NKCC1 comparator perturbations were weakly engaged in 151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut, so their failure to phenocopy Slc12a5 is a boundary on the mRNA assay, not proof that chloride-transporter signaling is absent.",
    "Fos, Dusp1, and Nr4a1 are general activity-responsive genes, not KCC2-specific markers."
  ],
